.\" Keep this page current: it describes the demo game demos/1987.bas (an executable made with BUILD, installed by "make install").
.TH ENGINE1999-1987 6 "November 2026" "Engine 1999 @VERSION@" "Engine1999 Manual"
.SH NAME
engine1999\-1987 \- 1987, a third-person adventure toy: walk and ride a bicycle through the countryside
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B engine1999\-1987
.SH DESCRIPTION
The year is 1987, a different 1987: after an alien invasion nearly all of humanity has been taken away, up into the ships or by beams of light, and only a few people are left on Earth. One of them is John, a lean, gray-haired former teacher of physics and mathematics who likes comfortable clothes. He lives alone in a small wooden house in the country. Next to it lies a large wheat field, ripe for the harvest, with nobody left to harvest it. Black, egg-shaped drones are said to hover over the land and shoot red beams at whatever looks suspicious; John only has his bicycle, and he loves it.
.PP
This is the first step of the game: the world and the two ways to move through it. There is rolling country with grass, sand roads, groves of pine, poplar and broadleaf trees, John's wooden house (with a porch, a yard with a fence, and a furnished room that you can walk into, with the bicycle too), a telephone line along the road, a scarecrow and the wheat field. You see John from behind (the camera follows him), as in the GTA games. John is drawn as a figure with a skeleton: his legs and arms bend while he walks and while he pedals, the bicycle's wheels spin, its handlebars steer and its pedals turn. High above the land hover black, egg-shaped drones. They scout: each one flies its own route over the countryside, the house and the field and sweeps a red beam over the ground below it (you see the beam and its red spot, and a shadow on the ground). The drones hum, a low note each that gets louder as they come closer. In this version they only scout: they do not shoot at anything yet. There is nothing to win yet and nothing is dangerous.
.PP
\fBThe bicycle\fR behaves like the bicycles in GTA San Andreas. It has to be pedalled up to speed (slowly at first; a sprint is quicker but tires John out: watch the stamina bar), it rolls on when you stop pedalling, a hill speeds it up and slows it down, the turning radius grows with the speed (a bicycle cannot turn on the spot), you can brake and then reverse slowly, and a wall or a fence at speed is a hard stop. You can get off at any speed; the bicycle rolls on by itself until it stops.
.PP
The program is the demo \fI1987.bas\fR of Engine1999, made into an executable with
.B BUILD
(window kind: it needs the SDL2 library, nothing else). The window can be switched between the full screen and a window with F11 or ALT+RETURN. The picture is drawn by the 3D mesh renderer, with OpenGL when the computer has it (see the settings), else by the software renderer.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP
.B "UP / W, DOWN / S, LEFT / A, RIGHT / D"
on foot: walk forward and back, turn. On the bicycle: pedal, brake (and then reverse), steer.
.TP
.B "SHIFT"
on foot: run at once (otherwise a held walking key makes John faster and faster up to a run; letting go slows him down to a stop). On the bicycle: sprint. Both use up the stamina (running only above a fast walk), which comes back when you walk slowly, ride normally or stand; when it is used up John only walks slowly and the bicycle only pedals normally until a quarter of it is back.
.TP
.B "SPACE"
brake (on the bicycle).
.TP
.B "E or RETURN"
get on the bicycle (stand within about two body lengths of it; a hint shows at the top of the picture) and get off again.
.TP
.B "B"
rings the bicycle's bell.
.TP
.B "Z and X, PAGE UP and PAGE DOWN, the right mouse button and dragging"
turn the camera round John and tilt it; it swings back behind him when you leave it alone.
.TP
.B "R and F, the right mouse button and dragging up and down"
look up and down without moving the camera (to see the drones); the view swings back when you let go.
.TP
.B "O"
opens the 3D settings: invert the X axis or the Y axis of the right stick, gamma, field of view, turning speed, the renderer (software or hardware, shown when OpenGL is available) and the render size. RETURN saves them (in the file 3dprefs.cfg of the folder ~/.local/share/1987/), ESC cancels, DELETE sets the defaults; changes show at once.
.TP
.B "P, Q or ESC"
pause, quit.
.PP
A game controller works as well: the left stick walks / pedals and turns / steers (on foot the deflection sets the speed: just past the dead zone a slow walk, fully pushed a run, with longer steps the faster he goes), the left trigger is an analog alternate to pushing the stick forward and works alone: pulled a part of the way it sets a target speed between a slow walk and a run (on the bicycle between a slow ride and the top speed, 30 km/h); on the bicycle the stick's deflection sets the target speed the same way (barely above the dead zone a slow ride, fully pushed the top speed, which needs stamina above plain pedalling speed) and the bicycle accelerates towards it; A brakes, B rings the bell, X gets on and off, Y opens the settings, the right stick turns the camera, START pauses, BACK quits.
There are sounds for the walk (a footstep at each step, different on grass, on a sand road, on wood and in the wheat), for the bicycle (the rolling noise of the tyres, louder with speed and on sand or in the wheat, the ticking of the freewheel when you roll without pedalling, the bell and a thud when you crash).
.SH FILES
.I ~/.local/share/1987/3dprefs.cfg
holds the 3D settings. Nothing else is saved.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ENGINE1999_NOBANNER
set to anything: no "Engine1999 proudly presents" opening at the start (the file config:nobanner does the same).
.TP
.B ENGINE1999_NOGL
set to 1: do not try OpenGL, always use the software renderer.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR engine1999 (1),
.BR engine1999\-basic (7),
